<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley meticulously arranged the files of several current and recent case files on her desktop, leaned back in her chair and heaved a breath infused with indignant resignation and determination. If Koalemos was taking the Nightclub Vampire case, well she\'92d take a bunch of his and revisit them. It was a formidable caseload, and despite expecting she\'92d be assigned new cases, she was determined to shadow Koalemos on that one as well.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was evident Koalemos had let Supaporn languish in PAPER\'92s Truth facility, never sparing him a second thought, which cast a shadow of doubt over his efficacy in handling the Jacobson abduction as well.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley began by looking into Supaporn\'92s records, and the more she looked, the more his records failed to be looked at. She\'92d never seen a person so invisible.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How could he have no armpit data?\'92 she mumbled to herself amidst swiping and scrolling through all the usual data swamps, amidst banging passwords into condescending text boxes, amid submitting to increasingly more invasive seven-factor interrogation procedures\'97eyeballs to cameras, fingers to sticky print-readers, hair and skin flakes to DNA-analysers. She swallowed hard as she resolved to access data swamps with even more humiliating identity identity procedures. These rarely had data of any value, and Hayley was sure the access requirements were deliberate pranks rather than valid authentication techniques. They sucked all kinds of things from the financial apps on her tablet which were deeply hooked into her health and gambling apps: blood pressure, heart rate, body mass index, blood alcohol fluctuations. They mined her preference for trifectas on alpaca races, and profiled her music streaming history.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Nothing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Haley gritted her teeth, adjusted her clothes and filled an abandoned coffee cup from the office kitchen\'97for the seedy authentication procedures that required urine samples. Many of them rejected her for being a caffeine addict.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There were only two possibilities.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Either: Supaporn lived nowhere, travelled nowhere, consumed no services legal or elicit, spent nothing, earned nothing, ate nothing, drank nothing, subscribed to nothing, never used a toilet, never had sex, was never healthy nor sick, never spoke, sang, coughed, bled, farted or, as far as the data could report, picked his nose.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Or: someone very powerful wanted Supaporn invisible.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The only evidentiary record of Supaporn\'92s existence was his recent starring role in 
\f2\i Curb your Individualism. 
\f1\i0 As Hayley prepared herself for another barrage of identity challenges to access the 
\f2\i Curb
\f1\i0  stream, she wondered about that. 
\f2\i Curb
\f1\i0  was funded by PAPER, so that ruled them out; they wouldn\'92t spend so much energy erasing Supaporn only to make him a celebrity on their flagship public relations show. Hayley doubted Police had the capability to redact Supaporn\'92s life, although perhaps Koalemos\'92s arms-dealing mother did.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s another thing,\'92 Hayley muttered to herself. Koalemos was involved in Supaporn\'92s arrest; perhaps he did personally arrest him. So why was Supaporn on 
\f2\i Curb
\f1\i0  rather than Koalemos\'92s own 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 ? Surely Koalemos\'92s ego would not permit his main competition to steal Supaporn away from his own show. Perhaps PAPER 
\f2\i was 
\f1\i0 orchestrating the whole Supaporn thing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley knew the Police had undercover agents inside PAPER, so she assumed PAPER would have agents inside Police. About all she could be sure of was Koalemos wasn\'92t one of them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley streamed the Supaporn sequence of 
\f2\i Curb
\f1\i0  episodes on triple speed. She was paying little conscious attention to details until something scratched her unconscious which demanded her attention by forcing her eyebrows to knit and her forehead to furrow. Reflexively surrendering to muscle memory. Hayley halted the stream, rewound and found the beginning of the current episode. Then she sped it through, watched, and slowed it down near the end. The to the start of then next episode, then to the end, and so on for a few more eps. In every one, Supaporn was grievously tortured yet, at the start of the next ep, he appeared unbruised and unbloodied.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley froze the stream and magnified Supaporn\'92s supernaturally healed face. \'91No make-up,\'92 she muttered. \'91The injuries were staged.\'92 She wondered over the possibilities. She grimaced and considered the possibility that Supaporn was complete fiction. The simplest answer to the complete lack of Supaporn\'92s breath analysis on any of the data swamps was that Supaporn never breathed, at least never as someone named Sukrit Supaporn.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And where was Supaporn now? Koalemos claimed the Elvi had busted him out of the most secure intelligence facility in the country. But the Elvi\'97known for working karaoke bars, and for keeping to and squabbling among themselves\'97hardly seemed to Hayley as likely freedom-fighters. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The more Hayley thought about it, the more the whole thing appeared staged, though she could posit no motive as to why.
